in the world of particle physics bigger  is better the bigger the detector you  can build the more you can see and the  bigger more powerful Collider you can  afford the more new particles you can  discover at the moment the world's most  powerful particle accelerator is the  Large Hadron Collider near Geneva  Switzerland  earlier this year scientists at the LHC  announced that they had found the Higgs  boson a new particle that is part of the  mechanism that allows all other  particles with mass but to really  understand the Higgs they want a new  Collider the international linear  collider the ILC will be 31 kilometres  long it will accelerate electrons and  anti electrons to near the speed of  light and create lots of Higgs bosons  for scientists to study but it will also  cost billions and with a global  financial crisis on most governments are  unwilling to pay Japan though might have  the political will to take on the  collider project at Keck Japan's high  energy physics laboratory scientists are  already busy testing parts of the new  collider  they are building superconducting  modules that will accelerate electrons  and anti electrons to high energies and  practicing focusing the particles and  too tightly packed bunches so that they  can smash into each other the work is  being done in collaboration with other  particle physics labs around the world  those nations could certainly help but  many scientists believe that only Japan  has the political will to actually build  the ILC at the moment both political  parties are willing to support the  project assuming they get enough help  from abroad  only time will tell whether this will be  the home of the next generation of  Collider
 

